Where the protocol comes from
-----------------------------

This program speaks to the same hardware as ykushcmd, the control application
Yepkit publishes for their YKUSH boards:

    https://github.com/Yepkit/ykush
    Copyright 2017 Yepkit Lda (www.yepkit.com)

The protocol this program speaks was implemented from Yepkit's published
documentation - the YKUSH3 USB control interface and I2C references, listed
with the dates they were retrieved in doc/README.md - and then verified against
a board. It was not derived from the source of ykushcmd. What the two programs
share is that protocol, which both must speak because the hardware requires it,
and the names of the command line switches. Facts and interfaces of that kind
are not what copyright protects, so this notice is here because the work
deserves crediting, not because a license compels it.

Nothing was taken from that source: no source file, no line of the help text,
no message the program prints, and no element of its structure. The two are
built differently. This one is a library with a command line front end, and the
board talks through a transport the tests can substitute; ykushcmd is a single
executable whose per-board classes inherit their HID access directly. Help and
output are written from scratch and were checked against every string literal
in ykush_help.cpp, ykush3.cpp and yk_usb_device.cpp - nothing overlaps.

Yepkit Lda has neither endorsed nor reviewed this work and does not support it.
Their trademarks and product names are used only to say which hardware this
program drives.

The behaviour differs from the original in these points, which may matter when
switching over:

  - only the YKUSH3 board is covered; YKUSH, YKUSH2 and YKUSHXS are not
  - the messages are worded differently, so a script that parses the output of
    the original has to be adapted
  - a command the board rejects exits with 1; the original never reads the
    answer and exits with 0
  - the state of the external 5V port is decoded, which the original omits
  - the length of an I2C read is transmitted as a single byte, correcting a
    conversion in the original that corrupts the report for ten bytes and more
  - a GPIO read prints its value instead of only setting the exit code
  - control characters in a device supplied serial number are replaced before
    the string reaches the terminal

The full and current list of deliberate behavioural differences is the table
"Where it differs from the C++ original" in the README.


Third party components
----------------------

No third party C library is vendored or linked. The program reaches the device
through Apple's IOKit framework, from Rust.

Every crate that goes into the binary is under MIT or Apache-2.0 terms; they
are listed with their licenses in THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md.
